{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01J6oCAGZzrgTsguen3rRcDh","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (2.4MB). Full output saved to: /home/runner/.claude/projects/-home-runner-work-OSS-A3-OSS-A3/b91c5a2a-7703-478a-afa9-a287785dded0/tool-results/bmx7ilz8q.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n1:[{\"number\": 1, \"title\": \"Will there be a license?\", \"created_at\": \"2020-01-23T19:34:37Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-01-26T15:31:06Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"First off, _AWESOME_ project. I'll definitely be tinkering with this.\\r\\n\\r\\nQuestion: will you be applying a license to the code? I'm not here to push any license, but I do think at least _having_ a license is a good idea.\\r\\n\\r\\nGood job on the project \\ud83d\\udc4f \\ud83d\\udc4f \"}, {\"number\": 2, \"title\": \"Great lib!  Trouble with width\", \"created_at\": \"2020-01-26T03:01:29Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-03-09T11:50:01Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"I'm really liking this library!  \\r\\n\\r\\nOne thing ... I seem to be having trouble with tables with widths greater than 80 chars.  Tried the various width options, but no luck.  Thoughts?\\r\\n\\r\\nThanks.\"}, {\"number\": 3, \"title\": \"Markdown tables\", \"created_at\": \"2020-01-26T16:28:59Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-05-28T13:58:04Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"Hi @willmcgugan! Looks like an awesome library. I'm really interested in using this for terminal rendering of Markdown files, but I need to be able to render Markdown tables. Is that something you think would fit in this library?\"}, {\"number\": 4, \"title\": \"Document whether Windows is supported\", \"created_at\": \"2020-01-26T19:07:41Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-01-27T16:23:38Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"I can't tell from the documentation, or the PyPI classifiers, whether Windows is supported. I'd like it if it were, but obviously that's up to you as a developer. But could you document more clearly whether you do, so that I don't have to download the library and try it out to check?\"}, {\"number\": 6, \"title\": \"README.md - table.add_column error in readme\", \"created_at\": \"2020-01-29T12:53:38Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-01-29T13:36:41Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"In readme there is a row that is wrong in the add_column.\\r\\n\\r\\nFrom:\\r\\ntable.add_columngit (\\\"Production Budget\\\", justify=\\\"right\\\"\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b91c5a2a-7703-478a-afa9-a287785dded0","uuid":"2000d4be-078c-4f4e-bffe-5190ff1dfd6f","timestamp":"2026-10-26T04:11:44.999Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"1:[{\"number\": 1, \"title\": \"Will there be a license?\", \"created_at\": \"2020-01-23T19:34:37Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-01-26T15:31:06Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"First off, _AWESOME_ project. I'll definitely be tinkering with this.\\r\\n\\r\\nQuestion: will you be applying a license to the code? I'm not here to push any license, but I do think at least _having_ a license is a good idea.\\r\\n\\r\\nGood job on the project \\ud83d\\udc4f \\ud83d\\udc4f \"}, {\"number\": 2, \"title\": \"Great lib!  Trouble with width\", \"created_at\": \"2020-01-26T03:01:29Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-03-09T11:50:01Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"I'm really liking this library!  \\r\\n\\r\\nOne thing ... I seem to be having trouble with tables with widths greater than 80 chars.  Tried the various width options, but no luck.  Thoughts?\\r\\n\\r\\nThanks.\"}, {\"number\": 3, \"title\": \"Markdown tables\", \"created_at\": \"2020-01-26T16:28:59Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-05-28T13:58:04Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"Hi @willmcgugan! Looks like an awesome library. I'm really interested in using this for terminal rendering of Markdown files, but I need to be able to render Markdown tables. Is that something you think would fit in this library?\"}, {\"number\": 4, \"title\": \"Document whether Windows is supported\", \"created_at\": \"2020-01-26T19:07:41Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-01-27T16:23:38Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"I can't tell from the documentation, or the PyPI classifiers, whether Windows is supported. I'd like it if it were, but obviously that's up to you as a developer. But could you document more clearly whether you do, so that I don't have to download the library and try it out to check?\"}, {\"number\": 6, \"title\": \"README.md - table.add_column error in readme\", \"created_at\": \"2020-01-29T12:53:38Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-01-29T13:36:41Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"In readme there is a row that is wrong in the add_column.\\r\\n\\r\\nFrom:\\r\\ntable.add_columngit (\\\"Production Budget\\\", justify=\\\"right\\\")\\r\\n\\r\\nTo:\\r\\ntable.add_column (\\\"Production Budget\\\", justify=\\\"right\\\")\\r\\n\"}, {\"number\": 7, \"title\": \"Console width off by one on some terminals / ssh\", \"created_at\": \"2020-02-04T10:07:43Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-02-04T18:32:13Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"On some consoles the output width seems to be calculated one character too wide. See the following example:\\r\\n```\\r\\n>>> from rich.console import Console\\r\\n>>> from rich.table import Table\\r\\n>>> c = Console()\\r\\n>>> t = Table(\\\"Yo!\\\", \\\"Tables!\\\")\\r\\n>>> t.add_row(\\\"a\\\", \\\"row\\\")\\r\\n>>> t.add_row(\\\"another\\\", \\\"row\\\")\\r\\n>>> c.print(t)\\r\\n\\u250f\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2533\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2513\\r\\n\\r\\n\\u2503 Yo!     \\u2503 Tables! \\u2503\\r\\n\\r\\n\\u2521\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2547\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2529\\r\\n\\r\\n\\u2502 a       \\u2502 row     \\u2502\\r\\n\\r\\n\\u2502 another \\u2502 row     \\u2502\\r\\n\\r\\n\\u2514\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2534\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2518\\r\\n\\r\\n>>> c = Console(width=c.width-1)\\r\\n>>> c.print(t)\\r\\n\\u250f\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2533\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2513\\r\\n\\u2503 Yo!     \\u2503 Tables! \\u2503\\r\\n\\u2521\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2547\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2529\\r\\n\\u2502 a       \\u2502 row     \\u2502\\r\\n\\u2502 another \\u2502 row     \\u2502\\r\\n\\u2514\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2534\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2518\\r\\n```\\r\\nI played around a bit and tested some combinations, some seem fine, others not:\\r\\n\\r\\n- ssh terminal in vscode: OK\\r\\n- ssh terminal in ConEmu: one too wide\\r\\n- ssh terminal in Windows command interpreter (Win+R, cmd): one too wide\\r\\n\\r\\nSource System is Win10, the system the console runs on was either Ubuntu 18 or Oracle Linux\\r\\n\\r\\nA possible workaround is as shown above: get a Console, and then get another one which is one narrower than the first one.\\r\\n\\r\\nLooking ath the code, i understand that you are retrieving the terminal size via `shutil.get_terminal_size()`, so maybe that issue is beyond the reach of the lib.\"}, {\"number\": 8, \"title\": \"Traceback output issue when code uses tab indentation\", \"created_at\": \"2020-02-24T14:15:12Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-03-03T17:55:05Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"![Cmder_2020-02-24_09-12-37](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/118418/75159368-fec45280-56e5-11ea-9883-4476dc1bdb0f.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nThat's what happens to the right border for the code lines when the code uses tabs for indentation.\"}, {\"number\": 9, \"title\": \"How to construct a console with styles?\", \"created_at\": \"2020-02-24T19:07:00Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-03-09T11:48:59Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"Hi! \\ud83d\\udc4b \\r\\n\\r\\nI've defined a console and theme like so. I think there's some out of date docs around constructing consoles, so I've read the code and this is what I got to:\\r\\n\\r\\n```python\\r\\nfrom rich.theme import Theme, Style as St\\r\\n\\r\\ntheme = Theme({\\r\\n    \\\"pass-tag\\\": St.parse(\\\"white on green\\\"),\\r\\n    \\\"fail-tag\\\": St.parse(\\\"white on red\\\"),\\r\\n    \\\"skip-tag\\\": St.parse(\\\"white on blue\\\"),\\r\\n    \\\"xfail-tag\\\": St.parse(\\\"white on magenta\\\"),\\r\\n    \\\"xpass-tag\\\": St.parse(\\\"white on yellow\\\"),\\r\\n    \\\"dryrun-tag\\\": St.parse(\\\"white on green\\\"),\\r\\n    \\\"test-location\\\": St.parse(\\\"dim white\\\"),\\r\\n})\\r\\n\\r\\nconsole = Console(theme=theme)\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nIs this the intended usage?\\r\\n\\r\\nP.S. Happy to make a PR updating the docs if so! :)\"}, {\"number\": 10, \"title\": \"Underscores are being stripped out incorrectly in table cells\", \"created_at\": \"2020-02-24T19:34:45Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-02-24T20:52:10Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"I've got a table which I created like so:\\r\\n\\r\\n```python\\r\\n    table = Table(show_header=False, padding=0, show_edge=False, box=0)\\r\\n    table.add_column(\\\"outcome\\\", style=theme, width=6, justify=\\\"center\\\")\\r\\n    table.add_column(\\\"location\\\", style=\\\"test-location\\\")\\r\\n    table.add_column(\\\"description\\\", style=\\\"none\\\")\\r\\n\\r\\n    table.add_row(outcome, location, f\\\"{description} {reason}\\\")\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nIf the `description` contains two underscores, such as in many Python function names, the underscores are removed.\\r\\n\\r\\nFor example, in the following screenshot, the top line is the description string. The bottom line is the description as displayed in the table.\\r\\n\\r\\n<img width=\\\"729\\\" alt=\\\"Screenshot 2020-02-24 at 19 33 08\\\" src=\\\"https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/5740731/75184571-82109300-573c-11ea-92be-0dd4fcbf7ca3.png\\\">\\r\\n\\r\\nThis *doesn't* happen if the description contains a single `_`. I was guessing it was trying to render it as Markdown or something, but I get the same issue when setting `markup=False` when initialising the `Console`. \\ud83d\\ude04 \\r\\n \"}, {\"number\": 11, \"title\": \"Setting up coverage reporting via CI / linting?\", \"created_at\": \"2020-02-24T20:34:35Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-03-03T16:10:24Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"1. Are you interested on having coverage reports via CI?\\r\\n\\r\\nI suggest using codecov for that (with a config that disables its comments, so that only PR/commit statuses are reported).\\r\\n\\r\\nI also suggest having a tox configuration for this then, so that `tox -e py38-coverage` could be used on CI, but also locally in the same way.\\r\\n\\r\\n2. Are you interested in having some linting via CI / tox also?\\r\\nFor that using flake8 might be good enough, but would require some fixes / running black/autopep8 once.\\r\\n\\r\\nI'd be happy to contribute whatever you prefer.\"}, {\"number\": 12, \"title\": \"Issue with `end` parameter\", \"created_at\": \"2020-02-25T18:33:51Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-03-09T11:50:17Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"I'm looking for a way to write something to my terminal without automatically taking a newline. With the `print` builtin, I can pass an empty string as the `end` paramter. The `end` parameter in `Console.print` doesn't seem to work in the same way however.\\r\\n\\r\\n**Example:**\\r\\n```python\\r\\nprint(\\\".\\\", end=\\\"\\\")\\r\\nprint(\\\".\\\", end=\\\"\\\")\\r\\nprint(\\\".\\\", end=\\\"\\\")\\r\\n\\r\\nconsole.print(\\\".\\\", end=\\\"\\\")\\r\\nconsole.print(\\\".\\\", end=\\\"\\\")\\r\\nconsole.print(\\\".\\\", end=\\\"\\\")\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output:**\\r\\n```\\r\\n....\\r\\n.\\r\\n.\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n**Expected output:**\\r\\n```\\r\\n......\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nIt also seems to add a space before the `end` character:\\r\\n\\r\\n**Example:**\\r\\n```python\\r\\nprint(\\\".\\\", end=\\\"x\\\")\\r\\nconsole.print(\\\".\\\", end=\\\"x\\\")\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output:**\\r\\n```\\r\\n.x. x\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n**Expected output:**\\r\\n```\\r\\n.x.x\\r\\n```\"}, {\"number\": 13, \"title\": \"`sep` parameter is inconsistent with builtin `print`\", \"created_at\": \"2020-02-25T18:41:20Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-03-09T11:50:36Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"`sep` behaves differently from builtin print. I don't know if this is intentional or not, but the behaviour is slightly different from how the docs describe it. (I didn't expect the `sep` to be added to the *end* of the string, only *between* the printed items).\\r\\n\\r\\n**Example:**\\r\\n```python\\r\\nprint(\\\".\\\", \\\".\\\", sep=\\\"a\\\")\\r\\nconsole.print(\\\".\\\", \\\".\\\", sep=\\\"a\\\")\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n**Output:**\\r\\n```\\r\\n.a.\\r\\n.a.a\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n**Expected output:**\\r\\n```\\r\\n.a.\\r\\n.a.\\r\\n```\"}, {\"number\": 14, \"title\": \"Progress bar functionality\", \"created_at\": \"2020-03-05T23:46:06Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-03-12T21:23:32Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"Any plans/thoughts on including progress bar functionality?\\r\\n\\r\\nI know they're another big pill to swallow, but they certainly could fit the premise of rich. Integrating outside progress bar libraries isn't great as it is right now.\"}, {\"number\": 15, \"title\": \"Add rich.__version__\", \"created_at\": \"2020-03-08T02:38:48Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-03-08T12:35:35Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"`rich` should have `__version__` property.\\r\\n\\r\\n```\\r\\nAttributeError: module 'rich' has no attribute '__version__'\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nThis is a common python convention to tell package version easily.\"}, {\"number\": 16, \"title\": \"rich.traceback -- do not wrap long lines\", \"created_at\": \"2020-03-08T02:45:44Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-03-08T13:09:52Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"`rich.traceback` makes stacktrace nicer but it wraps everything in 80 characters.\\r\\n\\r\\nFor example, see a screenshot in your blog post -- https://www.willmcgugan.com/blog/tech/post/better-python-tracebacks-with-rich/.\\r\\n\\r\\n```\\r\\nTraceback (most recent call last):\\r\\n\\u256d\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u256e\\r\\n\\u2502 File                                                                                             \\u2502\\r\\n\\u2502\\\"/home/USERNAME/.miniconda3/envs/longer-paths-for-reasons/lib/python3.7/site-packages/numpy/core/f\\u2502\\r\\n\\u2502line 1477, in squeeze                                                                             \\u2502\\r\\n    (... omitted)\\r\\n\\u2502\\r\\n\\u2570\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u256f\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nAs it truncates the full stacktrace line at certain characters, it becomes inconvenient for users to see which file to look at. I suggest we should not wrap them inside a box.\"}, {\"number\": 17, \"title\": \"Long string wrapping in table columns\", \"created_at\": \"2020-03-08T19:29:14Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-03-09T18:14:24Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"So, long strings (exceeding the width of the console) aren't wrapped and result in output like:\\r\\n\\r\\n```\\r\\n cf69f8a0618f3bb8ba0ed2cc31d730827b09c3ca\\r\\n\\r\\n README.md\\r\\n\\r\\n 2.91 KiB\\r\\n\\r\\n 16 KiB\\r\\n\\r\\n 1\\r\\n\\r\\n Yes\\r\\n 2020-03-08 15:27:37 -04:00\\r\\n\\r\\n thorod 2.0.0\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n magnet:?dn=README.md&xt=urn:btih:cf69f8a0618f3bb8ba0ed2cc31d730827b09c3ca&xl=2984&tr=udp://tracker.opentrackr.org:1337/announce&tr=udp://9.rarbg.to:2710/an\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nwhere the string is cut and the left column disappears instead of something like:\\r\\n\\r\\n```\\r\\n Info Hash:      212769dc7fdac4030d93b517720914fd575b3ce1\\r\\n Torrent Name:   README.md\\r\\n Data Size:      2.91 KiB\\r\\n Piece Size:     16 KiB\\r\\n Piece Count:    1\\r\\n Private:        Yes\\r\\n Creation Date:  2020-03-08 15:27:07 -04:00\\r\\n Created By:     thorod 2.0.0\\r\\n Comment:\\r\\n Source:\\r\\n Magnet:         magnet:?dn=README.md&xt=urn:btih:212769dc7fdac4030d93b517720914fd575b3ce1&xl=2984&tr=udp://explodie.org:6969/announce\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nShould rich be wrapping these itself?\"}, {\"number\": 18, \"title\": \"Markdown markup syntax interferes with some filenames\", \"created_at\": \"2020-03-09T15:04:40Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-03-11T13:19:56Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"Example:\\r\\n\\r\\n![Cmder_2020-03-09_11-01-14](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/118418/76227244-648c0080-61f5-11ea-9536-03c15d7b01c5.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nThese are dunder filenames from a python project directory. Backticks and asterisks are also possible in filenames.\\r\\n\"}, {\"number\": 19, \"title\": \"When CJK characters are included in the line, the width is displayed incorrect\", \"created_at\": \"2020-03-11T13:02:47Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-03-18T22:00:31Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/6257434/76419334-1df7fb00-63db-11ea-8d6a-9af56524f55d.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nBecause the width of a CJK character is equal to the width of two letters, special handling may be required.\\r\\nSee: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halfwidth_and_fullwidth_forms\"}, {\"number\": 20, \"title\": \"Console.print is broken\", \"created_at\": \"2020-03-14T17:09:16Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-03-14T17:30:40Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"[Screenshot](https://i.imgur.com/GOZrXCv.png)\\r\\n>>> from rich.console import Console\\r\\n>>> Console.print(\\\"Where there is a [bold cyan]Will[/bold cyan] there [u]is[/u] a [i]way[/i].\\\")\\r\\nTraceback (most recent call last):\\r\\n  File \\\"<stdin>\\\", line 1, in <module>\\r\\n  File \\\"/home/damon/src/virtualenv-16.7.6/bun/lib/python3.8/site-packages/rich/console.py\\\", line 740, in print\\r\\n    self.line()\\r\\nAttributeError: 'str' object has no attribute 'line'\\r\\n\"}, {\"number\": 21, \"title\": \"Yaml syntax highlight fails\", \"created_at\": \"2020-03-15T15:05:46Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-03-15T19:43:17Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"Trying to use `yaml` in syntaxt highlighting fails:\\r\\n\\r\\n```python\\r\\nfrom rich.console import Console\\r\\nfrom rich.syntax import Syntax\\r\\nconsole = Console()\\r\\ncode='''\\r\\nyaml: true\\r\\n'''\\r\\nsyntax = Syntax(code, 'yaml')\\r\\nconsole.print(syntax)\\r\\n```\\r\\n```shell\\r\\n$ python3 syntax.py \\r\\nTraceback (most recent call last):\\r\\n  File \\\"syntax.py\\\", line 9, in <module>\\r\\n    console.print(syntax)\\r\\n  File \\\"/home/todoesverso/.local/lib/python3.7/site-packages/rich/console.py\\\", line 752, in print\\r\\n    extend(render(renderable, render_options))\\r\\n  File \\\"/home/todoesverso/.local/lib/python3.7/site-packages/rich/console.py\\\", line 520, in render\\r\\n    yield from self._render(renderable, options)\\r\\n  File \\\"/home/todoesverso/.local/lib/python3.7/site-packages/rich/console.py\\\", line 496, in _render\\r\\n    for render_output in iter_render:\\r\\n  File \\\"/home/todoesverso/.local/lib/python3.7/site-packages/rich/syntax.py\\\", line 209, in __console__\\r\\n    text = self._highlight(self.lexer_name)\\r\\n  File \\\"/home/todoesverso/.local/lib/python3.7/site-packages/rich/syntax.py\\\", line 156, in _highlight\\r\\n    append(token, _get_theme_style(token_type))\\r\\n  File \\\"/home/todoesverso/.local/lib/python3.7/site-packages/rich/syntax.py\\\", line 125, in _get_theme_style\\r\\n    pygments_style = self._pygments_style_class.style_for_token(token_type)\\r\\n  File \\\"/home/todoesverso/.local/lib/python3.7/site-packages/pygments/style.py\\\", line 128, in style_for_token\\r\\n    t = cls._styles[token]\\r\\nKeyError: Token.Literal.Scalar.Plain\\r\\n```\"}, {\"number\": 23, \"title\": \"Progress: speed of 0 gets ZeroDivisionError\", \"created_at\": \"2020-03-26T19:57:50Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-03-27T18:50:40Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"\\r\\n```\\r\\nIn [9]: with Progress() as progress:\\r\\n   ...:     a_task = progress.add_task(\\\"thing\\\", total=41)\\r\\n   ...:     progress.start()                             \\r\\n   ...:     for cnt in range(41):                        \\r\\n   ...:         time.sleep(.1)                           \\r\\n   ...:         progress.update(a_task, 1)               \\r\\n   ...:                                                  \\r\\nthing \\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501   0% -:--:-- Exception in thread Thread-704:\\r\\nTraceback (most recent call last):                                                         \\r\\n  File \\\"/org/seg/tools/freeware/python/3.6.3/1/el-6-x86_64/lib/python3.6/threading.py\\\", line 916, in _bootstrap_inner\\r\\n    self.run()                                                                                                       \\r\\n  File \\\"/home/bphunter/.cache/pypoetry/virtualenvs/icmerge-0gw-uTeS-py3.6/lib/python3.6/site-packages/rich/progress.py\\\", line 289, in run\\r\\n    self.progress.refresh()                                                                                                              \\r\\n  File \\\"/home/bphunter/.cache/pypoetry/virtualenvs/icmerge-0gw-uTeS-py3.6/lib/python3.6/site-packages/rich/progress.py\\\", line 501, in refresh\\r\\n    self._live_render.set_renderable(self._table)                                                                                            \\r\\n  File \\\"/home/bphunter/.cache/pypoetry/virtualenvs/icmerge-0gw-uTeS-py3.6/lib/python3.6/site-packages/rich/progress.py\\\", line 521, in _table \\r\\n    widget = column(task)                                                                                                                    \\r\\n  File \\\"/home/bphunter/.cache/pypoetry/virtualenvs/icmerge-0gw-uTeS-py3.6/lib/python3.6/site-packages/rich/progress.py\\\", line 107, in __call__\\r\\n    renderable = self.render(task)                                                                                                            \\r\\n  File \\\"/home/bphunter/.cache/pypoetry/\"}, {\"number\": 24, \"title\": \"rich.logging undocumented?\", \"created_at\": \"2020-03-26T20:02:49Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-03-27T08:37:03Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"Your [blog post](https://www.willmcgugan.com/blog/tech/post/prettier-logging-with-rich) refers to `rich.logging.RichHandler`, which is present in the code, but doesn't show up in [ReadTheDocs](https://rich.readthedocs.io/en/latest/index.html).\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n\"}, {\"number\": 25, \"title\": \"CJK characters are abbreviated on right-aligned tables\", \"created_at\": \"2020-03-27T03:33:12Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-03-27T12:09:34Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/6257434/77718533-2298f200-701e-11ea-807d-78a0daf7bf66.png)\"}, {\"number\": 26, \"title\": \"Support for rich outputs in jupyter notebooks\", \"created_at\": \"2020-03-28T19:37:55Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-03-31T13:27:09Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"I was using rich for a ML project and find it super useful! Kudos for building this awesome project. \\r\\n\\r\\nI did have a feature request though\\r\\n\\r\\nCan you add support for printing into notebooks. Jupyter notebooks does support rendering HTML and your library does export HTML and I was able to hack together some awesome outputs but it would be cool to have native support. Especially some _repr_html_ for attributes like table text etc.\\r\\n\\r\\nI would love to help add this feature!\"}, {\"number\": 27, \"title\": \"Don't highlight all numbers\", \"created_at\": \"2020-03-29T09:02:22Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-03-29T13:14:48Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/6257434/77845127-dfd24800-71de-11ea-8f65-2ce4e0e53185.png)\\r\\n\\r\\nsuch as UUID, it is a good choice to only highlight numbers with spaces before and after.\"}, {\"number\": 29, \"title\": \"Too long console.log() will be truncated if there is a word at the beginning\", \"created_at\": \"2020-03-30T21:31:13Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-03-31T13:28:19Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/6257434/77963826-a4379b00-7310-11ea-97dc-7c4689312330.png)\\r\\n\"}, {\"number\": 30, \"title\": \"The background of too long code in the traceback may not be rendered properly\", \"created_at\": \"2020-03-30T21:47:00Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-03-31T15:57:14Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/6257434/77964557-d39ad780-7311-11ea-85ac-1752227e8899.png)\\r\\nI guess correct rendering won't let the number of rows be rendered?\\r\\n\\r\\nHowever, I actually hope that there is an option to ignore the excess of the long code, because I think that traceback is only to facilitate the location of the faulty code line, and it is not necessary to display everything.\\r\\nThe excess can be marked as an ellipsis at the rear.\"}, {\"number\": 31, \"title\": \"Is it possible to change row style\", \"created_at\": \"2020-04-11T13:06:14Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-04-12T12:52:51Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"While generating row's I'd like to set slightly different colour for every odd row, to make it easier to visually stay in line.\\r\\n\\r\\nI can do something like this to hack around it:\\r\\n```python\\r\\n    def row(self, table, *rows):\\r\\n        clr = \\\"grey93\\\" if self.row_number % 2 == 0 else \\\"white\\\"\\r\\n        rows = [self.color(clr, row) for row in rows]\\r\\n        table.add_row(*rows)\\r\\n        self.row_number += 1\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\n(self.color just wraps the string with the colour)\\r\\nThe problem with this approach is that as I'm not really setting the row style, I remove any existing colours that the row items may have, and I'd only like to change the default colour.\\r\\n\\r\\n\"}, {\"number\": 32, \"title\": \"Internal quote pairs cause unexpected coloring?\", \"created_at\": \"2020-04-11T17:58:49Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-04-14T14:41:28Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"Nice module!  \\r\\n\\r\\nI've noticed the following odd behavior: when using rich.print with bbcode tags, if the string contains matching quotes the substring between the quotes is colored green.\\r\\n\\r\\n```python\\r\\nfrom rich import print\\r\\n# Contains a quote pair, Malley is printed in green\\r\\nprint(\\\"[bold]O'Malley's Bar[/bold]\\\")\\r\\n\\r\\n# Only one quote, prints as expected\\r\\nprint(\\\"[bold]O'Malleys Bar[/bold]\\\")\\r\\n```\\r\\nI've tried this with a few different tags, swapping the internal quote types, and using Console.print() but all show the same behavior.\\r\\n\"}, {\"number\": 33, \"title\": \"Importing Console throws AttributeError in Python 3.6.0\", \"created_at\": \"2020-04-18T21:50:10Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-04-19T13:40:56Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"Hi, thanks for this wonderful library.\\r\\n\\r\\nI just randomly discovered this error:\\r\\n```\\r\\n$ ./venv36/bin/python -c \\\"from rich.console import Console\\\"\\r\\nTraceback (most recent call last):\\r\\n  File \\\"<string>\\\", line 1, in <module>\\r\\n  File \\\"/home/michal/python/SzT/venv36/lib/python3.6/site-packages/rich/console.py\\\", line 38, in <module>\\r\\n    from .default_styles import DEFAULT_STYLES\\r\\n  File \\\"/home/michal/python/SzT/venv36/lib/python3.6/site-packages/rich/default_styles.py\\\", line 18, in <module>\\r\\n    strike=False,\\r\\n  File \\\"/home/michal/python/SzT/venv36/lib/python3.6/site-packages/rich/style.py\\\", line 73, in __init__\\r\\n    self._color = None if color is None else _make_color(color)\\r\\n  File \\\"/home/michal/python/SzT/venv36/lib/python3.6/site-packages/rich/style.py\\\", line 71, in _make_color\\r\\n    return color if isinstance(color, Color) else Color.parse(color)\\r\\nAttributeError: type object 'Color' has no attribute 'parse'\\r\\n```\\r\\n\\r\\nPython versions 3.6.2 and 3.6.4 don't exhibit this issue.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe `Color` class clearly does have a `parse` class method. However:\\r\\n```\\r\\n$ ./venv36/bin/python -c \\\"from rich.color import Color; print(hasattr(Color, 'parse'))\\\"\\r\\nFalse\\r\\n$ ./venv362/bin/python -c \\\"from rich.color import Color; print(hasattr(Color, 'parse'))\\\"\\r\\nTrue\\r\\n$ ./venv364/bin/python -c \\\"from rich.color import Color; print(hasattr(Color, 'parse'))\\\"\\r\\nTrue\\r\\n```\"}, {\"number\": 34, \"title\": \"Progress Bar issue with rich==0.8.11\", \"created_at\": \"2020-04-21T08:48:18Z\", \"closed_at\": \"2020-04-21T08:50:38Z\", \"state_reason\": \"completed\", \"body\": \"Hello!\\r\\n\\r\\nFound a issue in the behaviour of Progress Bar starting rich 0.8.11:\\r\\n\\r\\n```bash\\r\\n> python --version       \\r\\nPython 3.7.4\\r\\n> pip list | grep rich\\r\\nrich                    0.8.11\\r\\n> python -m rich.progress                                                     \\r\\n0 0 0\\r\\nDownloading... \\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501   0% -:--:-- 0 0 0\\r\\n0 0 0\\r\\nDownloading... \\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501   0% -:--:-- \\r\\nProcessing...  \\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501   0% -:--:-- 0 0 0\\r\\n0 0 0\\r\\nDownloading... \\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2501\\u2